.TH "GIT-RHU" "1" "2026-08-17" "pylitfun" "Git-Verbs Manual"
.SH NAME
git-rhu, grhu \- roll back the working tree to what you last fetched
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git rhu
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git rhu
resets the current branch hard to its upstream, discarding every local commit and every local
change, by running
.BR "git reset --hard @{upstream}" .
.PP
Because this both moves the branch pointer and overwrites the working tree,
.B git rhu
prints the command it is about to run, then blocks reading Stdin until you press Control-D to
confirm (or Control-C to cancel), before it changes anything.
.PP
Unlike
.BR git-cl (1),
.B git reset --hard
has no dry-run flag, so there is no way to preview what
.B git rhu
would discard before confirming.
.SH NOTES
Some causes of divergence from the upstream are better undone before reaching for
.BR "git rhu" :
.RS 4
git rebase \-\-abort
.br
git cherry\-pick \-\-abort
.br
git checkout main
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
Exits with the status of the
.B git reset
it ran.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
git rhu
.RS 4
Confirm, then discard every local commit and local change, resetting to
.BR @{upstream} .
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-reset (1),
.BR git-cl (1)
